Initialize the xz decoder status to XZ_OK, not X_OK.
The status variable started out as X_OK, the access(2) mode from
unistd.h, rather than XZ_OK. X_OK is 1, which is XZ_STREAM_END, so the
loop believed a stream had just ended before reading anything. Empty
input then ended the loop as a clean end of data, and input whose first
nonzero byte was not the start of an xz header was taken for trailing
garbage after a stream. Either way nothing was decoded and the status
was success: xzcat printed nothing and exited 0, and unxz replaced a
file that was not xz data with an empty file and deleted the original.
Zero bytes before the first stream were also skipped as stream padding,
which the format allows only after a stream; they are now rejected as
corrupted data, as is empty input.

---
 archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c
+++ b/archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c
@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@
 	/* Limit memory usage to about 64 MiB. */
 	state = xz_dec_init(XZ_DYNALLOC, 64*1024*1024);
 
-	xz_result = X_OK;
+	xz_result = XZ_OK;
 	while (1) {
 		if (iobuf.in_pos == iobuf.in_size) {
 			int rd = safe_read(xstate->src_fd, membuf, BUFSIZ);
